        {
            "id": "21438205",
            "user": "Wikiphoto",
            "timestamp": "21:36, 2 December 2016",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(8,143 bytes)",
            "change": 0,
            "summary": "(rolled forward visuals)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "adjustment",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Understand what the job entails. Electricians work in a variety of settings, including homes, businesses, schools, hospitals - any type of facility that needs electricity to function. Electricians may also perform electrical work on trains, airplanes, ships and vehicles. Electricians' work includes the following:[[Image:Become a Licensed Electrician Step 1 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Have an aptitude for electrical work. Electricians are adept at diagnosing problems and using good judgement and the right techniques to solve them. Electricians have the following traits:[[Image:Become a Licensed Electrician Step 2 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Talk to electricians. If you're serious about becoming a licensed technician, contact electricians in your area and set up informational interviews.[[Image:Become a Licensed Electrician Step 3 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Obtain a high school diploma or General Equivalency Diploma (GED). Most electrician trade schools and apprenticeship programs require this level of education.[[Image:Become a Licensed Electrician Step 4 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Attend a trade school or vocational school. The courses offered at trade and vocational schools provide important preparation for entering an electrician apprenticeship program.[[Image:Become a Licensed Electrician Step 5 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Enter an electrician apprentice program. Most states require at least two years, and in most cases up to four years, of apprenticeship with a master or licensed electrician before one can take the examination to become a licensed electrician. During an apprenticeship, one earns the title of [[Become a Journeyman Electrician (US)|journeyman electrician]]. Many apprenticeships combine hands-on experience with classroom instruction. Some organizations that sponsor or provide apprentice programs through local chapters include:[[Image:Become a Licensed Electrician Step 6 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Study the [http://www.nfpa.org/aboutthecodes/AboutTheCodes.asp?DocNum=70&cookie_test=1 National Electrical Code]. In order to get licensed to work as an electrician in most states, you will have to demonstrate knowledge of the National Electrical Code, which lays out laws, regulations and other information about safe practices.[[Image:Become a Licensed Electrician Step 7 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Get a state license. Most states require that you get a state license in order to practice as an electrician. The license is granted after taking an exam to demonstrate knowledge of the National Electrical Code and local electrical and building codes. In order to take the exam,[[Image:Become a Licensed Electrician Step 8 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find a job. Now that you have your state license, you are free to work as an electrical contractor in your state. Choose to work with a company of contractors or operate independently.[[Image:Become a Licensed Electrician Step 9 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Get certified. Choose from a variety of specialized certifications to enhance your career. Certifications vary by state and will help you pursue work as an electrical administrator, a telecommunications contractor, or a specialist in an area like instrumentation.[[Image:Become a Licensed Electrician Step 10 Version 3.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Understand what the job entails. Electricians work in a variety of settings, including homes, businesses, schools, hospitals - any type of facility that needs electricity to function. Electricians may also perform electrical work on trains, airplanes, ships and vehicles. Electricians' work includes the following:[[Image:Become a Licensed Electrician Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Have an aptitude for electrical work. Electricians are adept at diagnosing problems and using good judgement and the right techniques to solve them. Electricians have the following traits:[[Image:Become a Licensed Electrician Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Talk to electricians. If you're serious about becoming a licensed technician, contact electricians in your area and set up informational interviews.[[Image:Become a Licensed Electrician Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Obtain a high school diploma or General Equivalency Diploma (GED). Most electrician trade schools and apprenticeship programs require this level of education.[[Image:Become a Licensed Electrician Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Attend a trade school or vocational school. The courses offered at trade and vocational schools provide important preparation for entering an electrician apprenticeship program.[[Image:Become a Licensed Electrician Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Enter an electrician apprentice program. Most states require at least two years, and in most cases up to four years, of apprenticeship with a master or licensed electrician before one can take the examination to become a licensed electrician. During an apprenticeship, one earns the title of [[Become a Journeyman Electrician (US)|journeyman electrician]]. Many apprenticeships combine hands-on experience with classroom instruction. Some organizations that sponsor or provide apprentice programs through local chapters include:[[Image:Become a Licensed Electrician Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Study the [http://www.nfpa.org/aboutthecodes/AboutTheCodes.asp?DocNum=70&cookie_test=1 National Electrical Code]. In order to get licensed to work as an electrician in most states, you will have to demonstrate knowledge of the National Electrical Code, which lays out laws, regulations and other information about safe practices.[[Image:Become a Licensed Electrician Step 7 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Get a state license. Most states require that you get a state license in order to practice as an electrician. The license is granted after taking an exam to demonstrate knowledge of the National Electrical Code and local electrical and building codes. In order to take the exam,[[Image:Become a Licensed Electrician Step 8 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find a job. Now that you have your state license, you are free to work as an electrical contractor in your state. Choose to work with a company of contractors or operate independently.[[Image:Become a Licensed Electrician Step 9 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Get certified. Choose from a variety of specialized certifications to enhance your career. Certifications vary by state and will help you pursue work as an electrical administrator, a telecommunications contractor, or a specialist in an area like instrumentation.[[Image:Become a Licensed Electrician Step 10 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
